KG42.register("rules-6b505a0cf2d3d217a2a9cfc6",{"id":"36-dsm_mechanism_design::v141-c-R004","paper":"36-dsm_mechanism_design","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R004","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","name":"Social-welfare clearing objective","process_stage":"clearing","market_meaning":"Marginal-cost price coordination with price-taking users yields a competitive-equilibrium allocation that maximizes social welfare. Scope: Price-taking users coordinated by marginal-cost prices at competitive equilibrium. Condition: Users are price takers, marginal-cost pricing is employed, and Properties 1–4 and Assumptions 1–3 hold.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function clearing.social_welfare under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.social_welfare","coordination.price_signal"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R004-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"clearing.social_welfare","type":"implies","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R004-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e007","v141-b-g02-e031"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Price-taking users coordinated by marginal-cost prices at competitive equilibrium.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e007","v141-b-g02-e031"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R004-xn","symbol":"\\mathbf{x}_n","meaning":"Power-consumption vector of user n over all time slots, with k-th component x_n^k; it is the user-n component of the aggregate consumption profile x=(x_1,...,x_N).","kind":"operational","domain":"\\mathcal{X}_n, the feasible energy consumption controlling set for user n in the competitive-equilibrium condition (12)","unit":"kW","indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e007"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-lambda-k","symbol":"\\lambda_k","meaning":"Price in time slot k. In the price-taking regime the price vector lambda=(lambda_1,...,lambda_K) is treated as fixed by each user; in a competitive equilibrium lambda is the price vector component paired with x.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"per-slot price component; in a competitive equilibrium it is the price vector component paired with x"},"unit":"dollars per kW","indices":["k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e007","v141-b-g02-e031"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R004-U-n","symbol":"U_n","meaning":"Utility function of user n, representing the utility of its total power consumption over the operation period.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"user-specific utility function U_n(.)"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e007"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Marginal-cost price coordination with price-taking users yields a competitive-equilibrium allocation that maximizes social welfare. Scope: Price-taking users coordinated by marginal-cost prices at competitive equilibrium. Condition: Users are price takers, marginal-cost pricing is employed, and Properties 1–4 and Assumptions 1–3 hold.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e007","v141-b-g02-e031"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"A pair (x, lambda) is a competitive equilibrium in the price-taking regime when each user n maximizes its payoff P_n(x_n) at the given price vector lambda over its feasible set X_n, with P_n(x_n)=U_n(sum_k x_n^k)-sum_k lambda_k x_n^k.","expression":"P_n(\\mathbf{x}_n) \\ge P_n(\\bar{\\mathbf{x}}_n),\\quad \\forall \\bar{\\mathbf{x}}_n\\in\\mathcal{X}_n,\\ \\forall n\\in\\mathcal{N};\\qquad P_n(\\mathbf{x}_n)=U_n\\!\\left(\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{K}}x_n^k\\right)-\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{K}}\\lambda_k x_n^k.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R004-xn"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R004-lambda-k","v141-c-R004-U-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e005","v141-b-g02-e006","v141-b-g02-e007"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e007","location":{"location_id":"src-0fb1d9d41e-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-D-1 Price Taking Users","anchor_text":"p , , p equilibrium if each user maximizes its own payoff function deﬁned in (11) for a given price ","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We call a pair \\((\\mathbf x,\\boldsymbol\\lambda)\\), where \\(\\mathbf x\\triangleq(\\mathbf x_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf x_N)\\), a competitive equilibrium if each user \\(n\\) maximizes its own payoff function defined in (11) for a given price vector \\(\\boldsymbol\\lambda\\), i.e.,\n\\[ P_n(\\mathbf x_n)\\ge P_n(\\bar{\\mathbf x}_n),\\quad \\bar{\\mathbf x}_n\\in\\mathcal X_n,\\ n\\in\\mathcal N, \\tag{12} \\]\nwhere vector \\(\\mathbf x\\) is the solution to the problem defined in (10). It has been shown that under Properties 1–4 and Assumptions 1–3, a competitive equilibrium always exists [20], [34], and the results are summarized in the following proposition.\nProposition 1: There exists a competitive equilibrium \\((\\mathbf x,\\boldsymbol\\lambda)\\), where \\(\\mathbf x\\) is an optimal solution to problem (10)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","location":{"location_id":"src-0fb1d9d41e-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV-B The Impact of Reflecting the Generating Cost","anchor_text":"cial welfare. Maximizing the aggregate utility of all users while minimizing the cost imposed on the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The proposed VCG mechanism is used to maximize the social welfare. Maximizing the aggregate utility of all users while minimizing the cost imposed on the energy provider is beneficial for both users and energy provider. ... It has been shown that in a system with price taking users, marginal cost pricing not only maximizes the social welfare, but also maximizes the payoff of the energy provider [20]. As an upper bound on the payoff of the energy provider, we consider a system which has price taking users and employs marginal cost pricing."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-0fb1d9d41e-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-D Problem Formulation and Efficient Allocations","anchor_text":"objectives with the social objective, some elaborately designed pricing scheme is needed. In general","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Given a price vector \\(\\boldsymbol\\lambda=(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_K)\\), where \\(\\lambda_k\\) is the price in time slot \\(k\\), a user who consumes \\(x_n^k\\) kW electricity in time slot \\(k\\) is charged \\(\\lambda_k x_n^k\\) dollars for that time slot."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-0fb1d9d41e-p05-c1r1__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-D-1 Price Taking Users","anchor_text":"1174 time slot. Since users treat the prices as ﬁxed values, the payoff function for each user becom","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Since users treat the prices as fixed values, the payoff function for each user \\(n\\) becomes\n\\[ P_n(\\mathbf x_n)=U_n\\left(\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}x_n^k\\right)-\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal K}\\lambda_k x_n^k, \\tag{11} \\]\nwhere the first term represents the utility of user \\(n\\) as a function of its power consumption, and the second term represents its payment to the energy provider."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M02-clearing","v141-a-M02-coordination","v141-a-M02-pricing","v141-a-M02-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"clearing.social_welfare","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","quote":"It has been shown that in a system with price taking users, marginal cost pricing not only maximizes the social welfare, but also maximizes the payoff of the energy provider [20]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e007","quote":"It has been shown that under Properties 1–4 and Assumptions 1–3, a competitive equilibrium always exists [20], [34], and the results are summarized in the following proposition.\nProposition 1: There exists a competitive equilibrium \\((\\mathbf x,\\boldsymbol\\lambda)\\), where \\(\\mathbf x\\) is an optimal solution to problem (10)."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"e2f8006d78d1b307ceb5eb4596b076b8f5e73451e5c6fff8530e331efcebdc88","applicability":"needs_revision","reason":"C001's social-welfare maximization implication cannot be fully transcribed from the allowed same-mechanism evidence. Evidence v141-b-g02-e007 supports the competitive-equilibrium payoff condition and Proposition 1's reference to problem (10), and v141-b-g02-e031 states the marginal-cost/social-welfare result only in prose. The supplied evidence catalog does not provide the complete problem (10) objective, the cost-function notation, or the marginal-cost pricing equation. C001 is therefore retained as not_formalized; a source-supported competitive-equilibrium payoff condition is added as C002. All B evidence remains unreviewed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-xn","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001"],"rationale":"x_n is the user-n consumption/allocation vector selected in clearing. It is used in the competitive-equilibrium payoff condition C002, which supports the frozen edge from the price signal to the social-welfare clearing outcome."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-lambda-k","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001"],"rationale":"lambda_k is the per-slot price signal. Price-taking users treat the price vector as fixed, and the price signal is the source node of frozen edge E001 into clearing.social_welfare."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-U-n","node_ids":["clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001"],"rationale":"U_n is the utility function entering the price-taking payoff and the social-welfare objective represented by clearing.social_welfare. It connects the clearing allocation to welfare on edge E001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001"],"rationale":"C001 remains not_formalized and corresponds to the qualitative implication stored by frozen edge E001. No expression is assigned because the complete problem (10) objective and marginal-cost price relation are not present in the allowed evidence."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R004-C002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.social_welfare"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R004-E001"],"rationale":"C002 is a source-supported transcription of the competitive-equilibrium payoff inequality. It uses the price signal on the source side of E001 and defines the allocation condition associated with the clearing welfare node on the target side."}],"revision_requests":["C.1: add a same-mechanism evidence record for the complete objective of problem (10), including the aggregate utility term and the energy-provider cost term with its sign. Evidence v141-b-g02-e007 cites problem (10), and v141-b-g02-e031 states the welfare result only in prose.","C.1: add the source equation defining marginal-cost pricing in the price-taking regime, showing how lambda_k is determined from marginal cost, or explicitly mark that no formula is supplied. Evidence v141-b-g02-e031 does not give the pricing equation.","C.1: clarify whether Proposition 1 is existential or universal for C001. Evidence v141-b-g02-e007 states that there exists a competitive equilibrium (x, lambda) whose x is optimal for problem (10); C001's wording does not distinguish existence of such an equilibrium from optimality of every competitive equilibrium.","C.1: provide the exact feasible-set definition X_n and cost-function notation if a full welfare-maximization expression is to be written. The frozen graph currently has no typed representation of these source objects."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
